   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to extending\nthe authority of the department of environmental conservation to manage the\nAmerican eel",
    "basePrintNo": "S7863",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7863-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0339-a, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7863\n\nSPONSOR: BYNOE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nextending the authority of the department of environmental conservation\nto manage the American eel\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the authority of the Department of\nEnvironmental Conservation (DEC) to manage American eel.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends Environmental Conservation Law (ECL) §\n13-0339-a(1)(b) to extend, until December 31, 2028, DEC's authority to\nfix by regulation measures for the management of American eel (Anguilla\nrostrata).\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority for American eel is necessary in order to\npreserve the current regulatory program. Failure to extend the existing\nregulatory authority by December 31, 2025 will result in the loss of\nregulatory protection for this species.\n\nThis bill would extend DEC's regulatory authority for American eel for\nan additional three years and ensure that DEC continues to comply with\nthe Interstate Fishery Management Plan (FMP) for American Eel (Anguilla\nrostrata) adopted by Atlantic States Marine Fisheries Commission in\n1999, and last amended in 2014. ECL § 13-0105, which sets forth the\nmarine fisheries conservation and management policy, provides that one\nof the State's objectives shall be the management of the State's trans-\nboundary and migratory species consistent with any interjurisdictional\nmanagement plans, interstate or state-federal. New York must have the\nability to regulate the taking of American eel in New York waters\nconsistent with the FMP. Moreover, failure to make necessary regulatory\nchanges as may be required by the FMP could result in the closure of New\nYork's American eel fishery.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority was last extended by Chapter 296 of the laws\nof 2022.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
